  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Let $a=-47$ and $n=6.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$",
  "worked_solution": "Dividing $-47$ by $6$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n-8<-47÷6<-7.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=-8.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=-47-(-8)(6)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-47=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-47={{(-8)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 6+{{1}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r=-8\\cdot 1=-8.$",
